# Security & Auth — ${PROJECT_NAME}

> Stability: **stable**. Changes via ADR.

## 1. Threat model (summary)

| Threat | Mitigation |
| :--- | :--- |
| Token theft via XSS | Strict CSP + httpOnly cookies (if edge layer) or in-memory tokens; no long-lived `localStorage` tokens |
| CSRF | `SameSite=Strict` cookies + double-submit token (when cookie-based) |
| Privilege escalation | Impossible client-side: authorization is 100% server-side |
| Data exfiltration | Audit log; no bulk dumps via UI |
| Replay | Short-lived tokens + refresh + `Idempotency-Key` on mutations |
| Clickjacking | `frame-ancestors 'none'` |
| Supply chain | Lockfile committed, CI audit, Renovate/Dependabot, pinned majors |

## 2. Authentication

### 2.1 Standard login flow

1. UI shows login form (email + password [+ MFA TOTP if `mfaRequired`]).
2. `POST ${LOGIN_ENDPOINT}` → `{ accessToken, refreshToken?, mfaRequired?: true }`.
3. If `mfaRequired` → OTP form → `POST ${MFA_VERIFY_ENDPOINT}`.
4. Tokens stored in memory (preferred) or httpOnly cookie via edge.
5. `GET ${ME_ENDPOINT}` loads profile + permissions into auth store.
6. Redirect to default landing route (or `returnTo`).

### 2.2 Refresh

- Access-token TTL ≤ 15 min (recommended).
- A single automatic refresh attempt per 401; failure → hard logout.

### 2.3 Logout

- `POST ${LOGOUT_ENDPOINT}` revokes refresh on the SSoT.
- Client clears in-memory tokens and invalidates the entire server-state cache.

### 2.4 MFA

- TOTP default, backup codes managed by the SSoT.
- Enforce for privileged roles starting at the agreed milestone.

## 3. Token storage matrix

| Option | Pros | Cons | When |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| httpOnly cookie + CSRF (via edge layer) | XSS-resistant; simple SSR/refresh | Requires edge layer | **Preferred** with edge layer |
| In-memory token | No edge required; no cookie | Lost on refresh → silent refresh or re-login | When no edge layer |
| `localStorage` | Persistent | Vulnerable to XSS | **Forbidden** for long-lived tokens |
| `sessionStorage` | Persistent per-tab | Same XSS surface | **Forbidden** |

Operational decision recorded in an ADR.

## 4. Authorization (RBAC / ABAC)

### 4.1 Model

- `Permission` = string `domain:action[:scope]` (e.g., `device:command:send`).
- `Role` = bundle of permissions.
- `RoleAssignment` = `subject ↔ role` with optional scope.
- The SSoT resolves and returns the effective list via `${ME_ENDPOINT}`.

### 4.2 Client usage

```ts
// cosmetic hide
{hasPermission('device:command:send', { scope: `fleet:${fleetId}` }) && <SendCommandButton />}

// route protection
export const loader = requirePermission('audit:read');
```

- A `<RequirePermission>` component / equivalent guards conditional rendering.
- Loader / route guard protects whole pages.
- **Never** make business decisions based on permissions in the client.

### 4.3 Authorization failures

- 403 → explanatory empty state with `correlationId` and "Request access" CTA.
- Role changes require a session reload.

## 5. CSRF

- Cookie-based auth → double-submit token in `X-CSRF-Token`.
- In-memory tokens → server-side `Origin` check; CSRF token not strictly required.

## 6. Content Security Policy (target)

```
default-src 'self';
script-src 'self' 'nonce-<NONCE>';
style-src 'self' 'nonce-<NONCE>';
img-src 'self' data: blob: https://<asset-domain>;
connect-src 'self' https://${API_HOST};
font-src 'self' data:;
frame-ancestors 'none';
base-uri 'self';
form-action 'self';
upgrade-insecure-requests;
```

- Nonce injected by the edge layer if present.
- No `'unsafe-inline'`, no `'unsafe-eval'`.

## 7. Additional security headers

- `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload`
- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`
- `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`
- `Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()`
- `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin`

## 8. Secret handling

- Never commit secrets.
- `.env.example` documents expected variables; real values via secret manager.
- API keys / cloud-storage credentials shown **once** at creation, last-four after.
- Dedicated rotation endpoint, audit-logged.

## 9. Audit for admin actions

Actions requiring **explicit confirmation** (typing the resource name):

- hard delete
- token rotation / revoke
- role promotion to highly privileged roles
- destructive rollout / publish
- bulk delete above N items

Actions requiring a **mandatory reason** (free text, audited):

- override of derived values
- hard delete
- privilege impersonation
- reversal of automatic classifications

## 10. Dependencies and supply chain

- Lockfile (e.g., `package-lock.json` / `Cargo.lock` / `pnpm-lock.yaml`) committed.
- CI: audit failing on `high`/`critical`.
- SRI for any external assets.
- No third-party scripts from untrusted CDNs.

## 11. Client logging and telemetry

- Shared logger (`<path>/logger.<ext>`); levels `debug | info | warn | error`.
- In production: only `warn | error` sent to the sink (env-configurable).
- Never log tokens, sensitive payloads, secret-bearing bodies.
- Automatic masking of `password`, `token`, `apiKey`, `secret` keys in log payloads.

## 12. Privacy

- No third-party trackers by default.
- Telemetry opt-in via runtime config.
- `${SESSIONS_REVOKE_ALL_ENDPOINT}` reachable from the profile page.

## 13. Per-PR security checklist

- [ ] No hard-coded secret
- [ ] No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (or equivalent) without sanitization + comment
- [ ] No new `eval`, `new Function`, `setTimeout(string)`
- [ ] No new domain in CSP `connect-src` without ADR
- [ ] All mutations carry `Idempotency-Key`
- [ ] All destructive actions confirmed and audited
